The second kind is knowledge by presence (*ʿilm al-huduri*). It does not come from outside and needs no concept to carry it; it is with the soul already. The plainest case is our knowledge of our own self. Hunger is another, and so are pain and anxiety. Anxiety has a cause outside us, yet the feeling itself arises inside the soul.

Knowledge by presence is [-complete and sure in a way that acquired knowledge is not.-]{+complete.+} When the body is hungry, the soul knows it at once and without effort, and nothing of the hunger is missed. The hunger may even be false, and yet what is felt is real. A person[-who has eaten enough can still feel hungry. They-] may be wrong to think that their body needs food. They cannot be wrong that they feel hungry.

Knowledge by presence is what the mystics are after. Sheikh Bahmanpour [-defines mysticism as-]{+gives a definition of mysticism:+} the urge to reach out to the infinite through knowledge by presence. The two kinds of knowing aim at different things: acquired knowledge (*ʿilm*) aims at understanding, and knowledge by presence (*maʿrifah*) aims at arriving. A person does not try to understand their hunger; they arrive at it and feel it. In the same way, the mystic's aim is to arrive at God, not to define Him. A definition draws a limit, and God has none.

