::: {.box-word title="Word Study: ʿIlm al-husuli and ʿilm al-huduri"}
*Husul* means acquiring or obtaining, whereas *hudur* means presence. In acquired knowledge (*ʿilm al-husuli*), what reaches the mind is a concept or an image of the thing, while the thing itself stays outside. In knowledge by presence (*ʿilm al-huduri*), the thing known is itself present to the soul, as our own self, our hunger, and our pain are present. {+The phrase+} Imam [-Khomeini's word-]{+Khomeini uses+} for the gnostic's [-witnessing (*huduriyya*)-]{+witnessing, *mushahada huduriyya* (witnessing by presence),+} comes from the same root. [-The-]{+Sheikh Bahmanpour notes that the+} religious texts use *ʿilm* and *maʿrifah* for the same [-thing; this-]{+thing, whereas Imam Khomeini writes of "an evident difference" between *ʿirfan* and *ʿilm* [@forty_hadith, p. 542]. This+} book keeps [-them-]{+the two+} apart[-only-] to make the two kinds of knowing easier to see. Chapter One's Word Study noted that there is no way to God except through knowledge, and left open what type of knowledge it refers [-to; it assuredly refers to-]{+to. If *maʿrifah* is arriving, as this chapter describes it, that knowledge is+} knowledge by presence.
:::

## Where reason stops
@@ -49,26 +49,29 @@ Reason cannot reach God Himself, so He must be known within the soul.
1. Reason knows a thing's attributes, never the thing itself.
2. If reason stops short of ordinary things, it stops further short of God, who has no colour or size for the mind to hold.
3. Imam ʿAli says that neither the range nor the depth of reason reaches God, who has no boundaries.
