{+6.+} Therefore: we know by presence only what lies within the soul, where a faint sense of God already lives, so God is to be known within the soul.
:::

Acquired knowledge is the work of reason (*ʿaql*), the faculty by which we understand and argue. Reason has a limit. When we know a thing, we know its attributes: its colour, its size, and what it does. Every description says that it is brown or square or useful, and none says what the "it" is. Ibn Sina, the [-greatest-]{+master+} of [-the Muslim philosophers,-]{+philosophy,+} granted this. The reality of things, he held, is beyond the reach of human reason.

If reason cannot reach what the things around us are, it falls still further short of God. God has no colour or size for the mind to take hold of. Imam ʿAli (as) [-says as much-]{+speaks to this+} at the opening of the first oration in *Nahj al-Balagha*, where he praises God.

::: {.quote [-ar="[VERIFY: Arabic of Nahj 1.1.1 from Qutbuddin p. 121]"-]{+ar="لا يدركه بعد الهمم ولا يناله غوص الفطن"+} source="Nahj al-Balagha"}
Soaring thoughts cannot reach him and deep minds cannot fathom him. [@nahj, 1.1.1]
:::

[-Imam ʿAli's-]{+The+} two images [-name the two strengths of reason. Soaring thoughts are-]{+can be read as+} reason at its widest [-range; deep minds are-]{+range and+} reason at its [-most penetrating. Neither arrives at-]{+deepest, and Imam ʿAli says that neither reaches+} God. The next sentence of the oration [-explains why. God, it says,-]{+continues: God+} "cannot be described, for he is beyond the limits of demarcated boundaries" [@nahj, 1.1.1]. A definition draws a line round its object, and there is no line to draw round God.

Reason still has work it can do. It can [-show-]{+argue+} that God exists, and it can say what He is not: [-He is not a body,-]{+God cannot be an object, and+} He is not in one place and absent from [-another, and He has no partner.-]{+another.+} All of this is knowledge about [-God, and all of-]{+God. Apart from the proof that He exists,+} it is negative knowledge: each statement says what God is not, and none says what He is.

ʿIrfan asks for the positive [-side: knowing-]{+side. We cannot know God's essence; what ʿirfan seeks is His attributes, known positively:+} God as the Merciful, the Compassionate, and the Forgiving. The names can be learned from a book. To know God as Merciful from within, as a hungry person knows their hunger, is knowledge by presence. {+Mulla Sadra held that knowledge by presence is the only way to know the reality of anything, and the only way to know God. The comparison with hunger shows how this knowledge is had, not how much is known. It has degrees, and in Sheikh Bahmanpour's words, "the more you know, the more bewildered you will become".+}

We have knowledge by presence only of what is within us: our own self and some of its states. A sense of God already lives there, however [-faint; Sheikh Bahmanpour calls it-]{+faint:+} a vague idea of God that we carry inside us. If God is to be known, and not only known about, the knowing must happen within the soul.

