## Forgotten together, known together
@@ -67,9 +67,9 @@ If the human story is the soul's story, losing sight of the soul is the deepest
Do not be like those who forget Allah, so He makes them forget their own souls. It is they who are the transgressors.
:::

The verse calls such people transgressors. Sheikh Bahmanpour counts most of us,[-practising-] Muslims included, among those who have forgotten their souls. He does not mean that we will {+necessarily+} be punished for it. He means that we do not realise the potential our souls hold, and so we do not develop them as we could.

The cure follows from the verse. If forgetting God makes us forget the soul, remembering God (*dhikr*) turns our attention back to it. This is how the mystics read the verse. The return comes in stages. A person first turns back to the self and sees that it must have a Creator. [-Full-]{+Fuller+} knowledge of the soul comes later, as the remembrance of God deepens.

Where remembering God brings the soul back, losing the soul takes faith with it. Surah al-Anʿam says so.

@@ -77,20 +77,20 @@ Where remembering God brings the soul back, losing the soul takes faith with it.
Those who have ruined their souls will not have faith.
:::

A ruined soul loses faith because its owner can no longer see it. A soul that is seen points to its Maker. The body is put together from parts, and a person can suppose that[-matter assembled-] it {+came about+} by [-itself.-]{+an evolution of matter.+} The soul is not [-put together from parts, so it cannot be put down to chance. As-]{+like that: as+} soon as it is seen, its Creator is seen.[-The lectures give one cause for every theory that puts the world down to chance: those who hold it cannot see their own souls.-]

Forgetting runs in both directions, and so does knowing. A saying handed down from the Prophet ﷺ, and from Imam ʿAli (as), puts it in a single line.

::: {.quote ar="من عرف نفسه فقد عرف ربه" source="Hadith"}
Whoever knows his own self knows his Lord. [@lantern, p. 9; [-@bahmanpour_slides]-]{+@bahmanpour_slides, Irfan 1: Theory, "Human Soul" slide]+}
:::

The saying holds because the soul reflects its Maker. God's qualities are infinite and differ greatly from the soul's. Yet there is a likeness, and through it God can be known by looking within. This does not make the soul a part of God. The soul carries something of the spirit (*ruh*) that God breathed into the first human being (Q 15:29). That spirit is something God created, not God Himself.

Knowing God therefore begins with knowing the soul. ʿAllama Tabatabaʾi [-draws-]{+ties+} this [-from-]{+to+} a verse that tells believers to take care of their own souls (Q 5:105). [-From it he concludes-]{+He writes+} that {+a believer who hears this verse with+} the [-seeker-]{+desire to remember Allah alone+} "must see Allah through the window of his own soul" [@light, p. 7].

