If a guide is needed, the next question is what authority he holds. The Sufi orders give one answer. Each order calls itself a path (*tariqa*) and is built round an elder with his own method of instruction, and each has a chain of masters (*silsila*) that it traces back to the Prophet ﷺ. Through that chain, the orders hold, the Prophet's inner knowledge passed from heart to heart, from each master to the next.

Shia agree that the Prophet's inner knowledge passed on, but hold that it passed by inheritance to the Imams (as).[-That leaves no room for a chain of masters.-] Imam ʿAli (as) received it as the Prophet's heir, not as one learned companion among others. The orders keep the idea of someone who receives [-the Prophet's inner-]{+that+} knowledge, Sheikh Bahmanpour observes, but do not [-hold that it passed to-]{+share the Shia belief in+} the Imams. {+Most of their chains still go back to Imam ʿAli (as), and some pass through Imam al-Rida (as) [@kernel, pp. 87–88].+} For Shia[-that authority belongs to-] the Imams [-alone,-]{+hold that place,+} and so, in his words, "We don't have such a thing as tariqa in Shia."

[-Without a chain of authority, a Shia guide cannot claim the obedience that the-]{+The+} orders {+also+} ask [-for.-]{+for total submission.+} In an order, a disciple (*murid*) submits to the master (*shaykh*) completely, whatever he says and whatever he does. {+As a rule,+} Shia travellers do not join an order. They find an instructor for themselves, one who comes from no order and holds no special licence to instruct. What they look for is a person they can trust, someone to whom "you could entrust your soul".

{+In this book's reading, the instructor the Sheikh describes is what+} ʿAllama Tabatabaʾi[-gives such an instructor a name and a place. He-] calls [-him the-]{+a+} general [-teacher: someone who-]{+teacher. Such a teacher+} has not been specially appointed to guide anyone. Consulting him is like consulting anyone knowledgeable, as a verse of Surah al-Nahl tells believers to do [@kernel, p. [-80].-]{+80; @light, p. 100].+}

::: {.ayah ref="16:43" words="9-"}
Ask the People of the Reminder if you do not know.
:::

The verse[-tells believers to ask those who know, and the general teacher-] is [-asked in this way-]{+general, but Tabatabaʾi sets a high bar+} for {+anyone+} a [-stretch of the road. In Tabatabaʾi's words, once "the wayfarer-]{+traveller submits to. A man+} is [-honoured by visions and-]{+not a teacher "so long as the+} revelations[-…-] of {+Divine Essence … are not manifested in him", and+} the {+revelations of God's+} Attributes and [-the Essence, his company is-]{+Names are+} not [-necessary"-]{+enough+} [@kernel, p. [-80].-]{+82; @light, p. 101]. Short of that rank, "one may not surrender oneself to everyone and anyone and submit and obey him" [@kernel, p. 83; @light, p. 102].+}

The special teacher is different. He [-names the special teachers as-]{+is one of+} those "specially designated to guide, and they are the Apostle of Allah and his true [-successors" [@kernel, p. 80]:-]{+successors":+} the Prophet ﷺ and the Imams [-(as).-]{+(as) [@kernel, p. 80]. "The wayfarer cannot dispense with the company of the special teacher in any circumstance whatsoever, even if he has reached his sought homeland" [@kernel, p. 80]. The company meant is inner, and not "merely their outward company and comradeship". It lasts through every stage of the road and after its end [@kernel, pp. 80–81; @light, p. 100].+}

[-The company of the Prophet and the Imams is the one the traveller never leaves. "The wayfarer cannot dispense with the company of the special teacher in any circumstance whatsoever, even if he has reached his sought homeland" [@kernel, p. 80]. The company meant is inner, and not "merely their outward company and comradeship". It lasts through every stage of the road and after its end [@kernel, pp. 80–81].-]A guide therefore walks beside the traveller under the Imams [-(as)-]{+(as),+} and never in their [-place: consulted and trusted for a stretch of-]{+place. A traveller submits to him only if he has reached+} the [-road,-]{+rank Tabatabaʾi describes, whereas the company that+} never [-obeyed as-]{+ends is that of+} the [-orders obey their masters.-]{+Prophet and the Imams.+} Chapter Fifteen returns to the Sufi orders and how the Shia path differs from them.

## Teachers [-within living memory-]{+of recent generations+}

::: {.point}
Shia can name teachers of this road in[-the-] recent [-past, none of them in a-]{+generations, and Tabatabaʾi's own line belonged to no+} Sufi order.

1. ʿAllama Tabatabaʾi named his own teacher, Mirza ʿAli Aqa Qadi.
2. Qadi's line runs back to Akhund Mulla Husayn-Quli Hamadani, {+one of+} whose pupils [-also-]{+first+} taught Imam [-Khomeini.-]
[-3. They were scholars-]{+Khomeini gnosis+} and [-jurists who stood outside the-]{+ethics.+}
{+3. Tabatabaʾi's line belonged to no+} Sufi [-orders.-]{+order; it is a line of teachers, not a chain of authority.+}
4. Therefore: these named [-teachers, outside the orders,-]{+teachers of recent generations+} taught the road this book describes, to God through the self.
:::

If Shia travellers find an instructor for themselves, there must be instructors to find. The tradition can name teachers who walked this road in[-the-] recent [-past.-]{+generations.+} ʿAllama Tabatabaʾi named his own teacher: "My preceptor was the late Haji Mirza Ali Agha Qazi" [@light, p. 106]. This is the same Mirza ʿAli Aqa Qadi who set beginners a year of the Musabbihat. Under him Tabatabaʾi travelled the path [@kernel, p. 8].

Qadi's line runs back two generations, to Akhund Mulla Husayn-Quli Hamadani, a different scholar from [-the Hamadani-]{+Mulla ʿAbd al-Samad Hamadani,+} who [-found-]{+saw+} the law, the path, and the truth in [-a single-]{+one+} prayer. Akhund Hamadani had been [-a distinguished student-]{+one+} of [-jurisprudence-]{+the outstanding pupils of the jurist Shaykh Murtada Ansari+} before he became a guide [@light, p. [-106].-]{+106; @kernel, p. 89].+} He trained several pupils who became guides in turn, among them Qadi's teacher and Imam Khomeini's first formal teacher in gnosis and ethics [@light, p. 106; @forty_hadith, p. 8]. The box below tells how Akhund Hamadani was himself trained.

Akhund Hamadani and [-those who learned from his-]{+the teachers of Tabatabaʾi's+} line were scholars, several of them jurists. [-They stood outside-]{+Of+} the {+many+} Sufi orders,[-and-] Tabatabaʾi [-is plain about it:-]{+grants that two or three are Shia [@kernel, pp. 87–88]. His own line belonged to none of them:+} "we belong to none of these orders and follow the directions of the late Akhund, who had nothing to do with these orders" [@light, p. 105]. {+He calls it "the chain of my preceptors" [@light, p. 106], but it is a line of teachers, one that "does not end in any of these silsilahs" [@kernel, p. 88], and not a chain of authority beside the Imams.+}

These teachers taught the road this book describes, which runs to God through the self. Akhund Hamadani taught concentration on the self, and all his disciples followed "the method of gnosis (ma’rifah) of the soul, which is essential to the gnosis of the Lord" [@kernel, p. 87]. Qadi taught the same way. He asked a traveller to set a time each day or night to attend to the self, "for half an hour or a little more" [@light, p. 107].

::: {.box-story title="Story: The weaver at the judge's door"}
More than a hundred years before ʿAllama Tabatabaʾi [-wrote,-]{+told this story,+} Sayyid ʿAli Shushtari was a leading scholar and judge in the city of Shushtar. One day a weaver knocked at his door and told him that a ruling he had given on a property was wrong. The property belonged to an orphan, the weaver said, and its deed was buried in a certain place. Shushtari found the deed where the weaver had said, and found that the witnesses had lied. The weaver returned[-on-] the next [-two nights-]{+night and the night after+} with the same [-warning, and on the-]{+warning. On that+} third {+night he+} told [-him-]{+Shushtari+} to sell his belongings, go to Najaf, and wait for him [-there.-]{+in the Wadi al-Salam after six months.+} Shushtari went, followed the weaver's instructions, and "reached a position too high to be described". In Najaf he attended Shaykh Murtada Ansari's lectures on jurisprudence and took over Ansari's teaching when Ansari died. Shushtari [-himself lived only six months longer,-]{+had long guided Mulla Husayn-Quli Hamadani, one of Ansari's pupils,+} and in [-that time-]{+the six months+} he [-trained Mulla Husayn-Quli Hamadani-]{+had left to live he persuaded him to follow his way+} [@light, pp. [-105–106].-]{+105–106; @kernel, pp. 88–89].+} Tabatabaʾi traces his own line of teachers back to the weaver through Shushtari, and adds that "it is not known who that weaver was" [@light, p. 106].
:::

## How to test a guide

::: {.point}
A true guide is [-known-]{+ruled out+} by {+breaking+} the law, {+shown+} by long company, and {+recognised+} by [-turning people towards God,-]{+where he turns people,+} never by wonders.

1. A guide's learning cannot show that he has walked the road.
2. Nor can wonders, which come early on the road, far from its goal.
3. The law can rule a guide out: anyone who works wonders while abandoning one command of God is lying.
4. How far a guide has travelled is shown only by long company with him.
5. Where long company is impossible, his teaching is weighed against the Qur'an, the Sunna, and the practice of the Imams.
6. [-Both tests are-]{+Care is+} needed {+either way,+} because pretenders turn people towards themselves, whereas a true guide turns them towards God.
7. The Prophet ʿIsa (as) gives this test in its simplest form: sit with one whose sight reminds you of God.
8. Therefore: a true guide is [-known-]{+ruled out+} by {+breaking+} the law, {+shown+} by long company, and {+recognised+} by where he turns [-people, and any believer can check the last.-]{+people.+}
:::

A line of true teachers does not make every teacher who claims a place in it true, so a guide must be tested. The question is whether he has walked the road himself. His learning cannot answer it. [-Knowledge of the road-]{+A teacher's writing or study+} does not [-come from study, so it cannot be read from what a teacher-]{+show whether he+} has [-written.-]{+made the journey.+}

Wonders cannot answer it either. [-ʿAllama Tabatabaʾi lists-]{+Tabatabaʾi's lectures list+} what cannot show that a person has reached the goal of the road (*wusul*): "extraordinary feats, knowledge of hidden things, of private thoughts of persons, passing over fire and water", and knowledge of the past and the future [@kernel, p. 81]. Such powers become possible early on the road, [-when the soul first begins to see what is hidden-]{+at "the beginning of spiritual vision"+} [@light, p. 101]. Yet "there is a very great distance between this point and the frontiers of wusul and perfection" [@kernel, p. 81]. A wonder [-shows that a person-]{+may come from someone who+} sees what others do not, [-and-]{+but it does+} not [-that he is-]{+make him+} a friend of God [-(*wali*)-]{+(*wali*): such feats are "not a sign of anybody's being a favorite of Allah"+} [@light, p. 101].

Where wonders prove nothing, the law gives a firm test. Najm al-Din Kubra, the master of one of the Sufi orders, gave a rule for the road that applies here. Someone may be seen flying in the air or walking on the sea, but if they have abandoned "even one injunction of God", then "you should know that they are liars". Their wonders may be real, but their claim to know God is false. <!-- src: L06 ¶18-20 -->

Keeping the law rules a false guide out, but it does not show how far a true one has [-travelled. Only-]{+travelled;+} time shows that. [-Tabatabaʾi writes that-]{+In Tabatabaʾi's lectures,+} the general teacher "is not recognized except through company and being with him in public and [-private". That company lasts until the traveller is sure of the teacher's reality and of the certainty he has reached-]{+private"+} [@kernel, p. 81]. A traveller [-comes to know-]{+must watch him closely "for+} a [-guide-]{+considerable time" [@light, p. 101],+} as anyone comes to know a person, by living alongside him. One of Akhund Hamadani's pupils, whose story is in the box below, lived alongside his teacher for fourteen years.

::: {.box-who title="Who's Who: Mirza Jawad Maliki Tabrizi"}
Mirza Jawad Maliki Tabrizi, "The most distinguished jurisprudent and perfect mystic" in his biographer's words, studied jurisprudence in Najaf under the great jurists of his day. There he came to know Akhund Mulla Husayn-Quli Hamadani, and he spent fourteen years with him, learning ethics and mysticism. He became a guide in his turn: the jurist Shaykh Muhammad Husayn Isfahani, himself an authority in learning and deeds, wrote asking for his instructions in ethics and mysticism. Maliki Tabrizi later settled in Qum, where he taught jurisprudence and ethics and wrote [@journey, p. 8]. He died in 1925, and three years later his ethics class was resumed by Mirza Muhammad ʿAli Shahabadi, the teacher of Imam Khomeini. When Shahabadi left for Tehran in 1936, he gave the class to Imam Khomeini [@forty_hadith, p. 8].
:::

Not every traveller can keep long company with a teacher before trusting him, and there is a test for that case too. Where it is hard to inquire into a teacher's state, the traveller should trust in God and weigh whatever the teacher says or prescribes "in the light of the Book of God and the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah and the practice of the Immaculate Imams" [@kernel, p. 83]. Only what agrees with them is put into [-practice.-]{+practice [@light, p. 102].+}

[-Both tests are-]{+Care is+} needed {+either way,+} because the road has many pretenders, who put their wares on display with claims to unveiling and inner sight [@kernel, p. 83]. Imam Khomeini names what they do. Those who style themselves guides of God's creatures and leaders of mystic orders "have distracted the attention of the creatures of God from Him, attracting them towards themselves" [@forty_hadith, p. 102]. ʿIrfan is sought for God alone, so the test of a guide is [-where-]{+whether+} he turns [-people. A true guide turns them-]{+people+} towards [-God, not-]{+God or+} towards himself.

The simplest form of the test comes in a saying of the Prophet ʿIsa (as). It is recorded in *Lantern of the Path*, the book attributed to Imam al-Sadiq (as). [-ʿIsa's companions-]{+They+} asked [-him-]{+ʿIsa+} with whom they should sit.

::: {.quote ar="[VERIFY: Arabic of the saying of ʿIsa in Misbah al-Shariʿa (Lantern of the Path, p. 13)]" source="Lantern of the Path"}
With one the sight of whom reminds you of Allah, … and whose speech increases you in knowledge, and whose actions make you desire the next world. [@lantern, p. 13]
:::

ʿIsa's three marks all ask where a person's company turns us: towards Allah, towards knowledge, and towards the next world. [-Checking them needs no inner sight, so any believer-]{+They are marks a traveller+} can [-apply this test.-]{+watch for over time, in the company he keeps.+}

Finding a guide therefore helps the traveller, as long as the guide is tested. A true guide has walked the road himself and walks beside the traveller under the Imams (as), never in their place. [-He is known by-]{+Breaking+} the [-law, by-]{+law rules him out,+} long [-company,-]{+company shows him,+} and[-by-] where he turns [-people, and never by wonders.-]{+people tells him apart; wonders prove nothing.+}

Once a guide is found, what does the road itself look like?

